WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:10.809
Andy Grove who was a Hungarian immigrant entrepreneur,
one of the founders of Intel, the CEO of Intel,

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:14.270
he was known as the guy that drove the growth
phase of Silicon Valley.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:19.720
I mean, this guy was respected among all CEOs.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:22.580
Some people would have called him even bigger
than Steve Jobs.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.090
Let me tell you, that's who Andy Grove is.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:28.300
He wrote a book once called Only the Paranoid
Survive.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:34.630
God bless his soul, he passed away early this
March, 2016, and those who knew who he was

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:40.200
and followed a lot of his content know the
industry lost a great man.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.220
If you haven't read his book Only the Paranoid
Survive, even though it's an old book, I highly

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.780
recommend everybody read it.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.890
The stories may not be relevant, but the concepts
will be relevant.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:54.430
I wanted to produce a video because paranoia
is a very interesting topic.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.280
A lot of people say it's good or not good
to be paranoid.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.420
I think there are 10 things to be paranoid
about as an entrepreneur, that are very critical.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.619
I think paranoia is absolutely necessary and
the ones who are the biggest thinkers are

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:10.320
usually the most paranoid entrepreneurs.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.610
The smallest thinkers are normally the least
paranoid.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:18.380
Now remember, I'm not talking about negative
paranoia, that everybody is out to get you

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.961
at the workplace and so you don't perform,
you don't talk, and you just kind of stay

00:01:21.961 --> 00:01:23.749
in a corner by yourself - that's small thinking.

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:28.039
I'm talking big thinking paranoia, like a
general would to do say the enemy is going

00:01:28.039 --> 00:01:31.210
to attack us from here, if they flanks us
here, we're good and protected, that's what

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:32.439
I'm talking about the level of paranoia.

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:34.630
So ten things, let's go through it.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.299
#1: In my opinion, in no specific order these
are ten things.

00:01:38.299 --> 00:01:41.619
#1: Speed of implementation.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:42.630
Speed.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:49.759
To me, speed is probably at the top of my
list of paranoia.

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:51.490
What do I mean by speed?

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:57.810
One, how quickly do we implement an idea that
we have that's very effective.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.859
I'm paranoid on the speed of that.

00:02:01.859 --> 00:02:07.119
Sometimes we're moving too quickly where it
become abrupt and we need to take our time

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:11.700
to be more diligent so we don't lose $728,000
on a project we're working on.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.700
Right?

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:19.090
But speed regardless is by far at the top
of my list of things to be paranoid about.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.489
Speed of delivery for customers.

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:24.790
Speed of delivery for partners, vendors.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.569
Speed on how quickly we get back to a customer
or anybody.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:29.569
Speed on emails.

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:31.310
Speed on placement.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:36.250
Speed on purchases made, how long does it
take to make the purchase, how easy is it,

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:37.250
right?

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.290
How quickly does the next step happen for
the product to be sent to them?

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:41.989
How quickly, if it's a loan, underwriting.

00:02:41.989 --> 00:02:46.189
If it's anything -- everything comes back
down to speed.

00:02:46.189 --> 00:02:50.739
The biggest companies in the world that become
way, way too thick became very, very thick

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:54.630
because at one point speed was very critical
and they've been flat because speed is no

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.060
longer a priority to them, okay?

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:57.440
You've got to keep that in mind.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:58.680
They forgot about the speed.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.980
They forgot about being paranoid about the
speed.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.890
#2: is time.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:06.290
What is time?

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:09.519
Time could be many different things, but I'll
explain to you what I mean by time.

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:16.239
Time to me is one, we don't have as much time
on getting this done as we think we do.

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:19.220
Let's go get it done, right?

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.659
How much time will it take for this to be
done?

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:26.000
So am I being diligent on the amount of time
it takes for me to get this project done?

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.510
Or are we just winging it?

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:34.000
We had a project that the technology guy told
me is going to take 90 days.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:40.360
Absolute lie to us and he got money, and everything
that was taking place, it's been nine months,

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:41.500
no delivery of a product.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.359
I'm not happy about that.

00:03:43.359 --> 00:03:44.590
Why did that take place?

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.909
Front end, there wasn't proper preparation
with this one developer that we hired and

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:50.500
we had to hire some other developer.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.390
And this developer sold himself very well
on what we did.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:57.989
I'd love to give his name away but I'm not
going to give his name away yet because if

00:03:57.989 --> 00:03:58.989
he delivers he's fine.

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:01.780
But sometimes it's the development of a product.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:04.689
Sometimes it's the time they say it's going
to take and it doesn't come through.

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:07.359
Sometimes it's someone telling us, "We can
only do it in 60 days," but they're lying.

00:04:07.359 --> 00:04:08.359
Why?

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:09.359
We didn't ask enough questions.

00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:10.359
We should have asked questions.

00:04:10.359 --> 00:04:11.359
So I can't sit and blame.

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:13.569
I should have taken responsibility and asked
more questions and our team should have been

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:15.030
a little more diligent.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.530
What can we do to be better with this time
part?

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.060
Another one with time could be how urgent
we are.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.280
How urgent are we about getting the answer
to a question?

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.050
Why do we take two weeks to get the answer
to this question, why can't we get that question

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.050
answered within two hours?

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.600
Why don't we contact the people to get that
question answered today?

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.860
I want the answer to that question today.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.900
So why is it taking such a long time to get
the question answered?

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.520
Even faster.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.100
We needed these three people to hire, why
are we taking so much time?

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:44.410
We needed to hire this person over here, we
needed to get this vendor, how come we're

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:45.410
taking so much time?

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:46.410
That's the time part.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:50.250
I'm paranoid about that part and I think entrepreneurs
who are not generally, everything becomes

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.750
lackadaisical and everybody else follows that
same exact culture.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:56.870
#3: Kind of goes with number two is deadlines.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:58.160
I'm paranoid about deadlines.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:04:59.960
Are we going to hit this deadline?

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:00.960
Is this thing going to get done?

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.660
It's similar, but it's kind of not, because
time can be so general.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:06.200
This is purely deadlines.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.610
Being paranoid about deadlines being met.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.389
You make an announcement that somethings going
to launch at this time, how paranoid are you

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:15.360
that this launch is going to take place?

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.270
How does the team feel about that deadline
not being met?

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.190
What are we doing about that part, right?

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.360
Paranoid about deadlines.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.090
#4: Internal systems.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:28.210
How efficient are your internal systems?

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.210
What type of product are your internal systems
producing?

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.980
Are your internal systems producing the best
type of product?

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.569
Is the experience the best type of experience
that it is producing?

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:42.849
What needs to be adjusted for it to be more
efficient, faster, more streamlined?

00:05:42.849 --> 00:05:45.820
Is it duplicateable, is it something that
anybody can do?

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.199
Is it a system that if you hire another person
can come immediately and do exactly what that

00:05:50.199 --> 00:05:51.949
person was doing for the last three and a
half years?

00:05:51.949 --> 00:05:53.660
Do we have a system like that?

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:58.361
Is it a system in a way where we hire four
employees, read this manual, got it, a week

00:05:58.361 --> 00:06:00.259
from now I can do the same exact thing.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:01.690
Is there a system for that?

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:05.259
How efficient am I with the systems internally
where everything's going to be working good

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:07.440
together and it's smooth, there are no glitches.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:08.800
How great is that system?

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.560
That's the system, right that you're paying
attention to?

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.230
The next one is is innovation.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.470
There's a lot of paranoia in innovation because
again, big companies are not paranoid about

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.889
innovation because they don't ever think they'll
be passed up.

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:24.610
Big companies are typically extremely arrogant
and cocky and we're the best and we're gonna

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.669
this, and they're just more from there and
usually a younger company comes and says we're

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:32.970
not the best, we are not cocky, we're not
there yet, you are bigger than us, you are

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.660
better than us, but man, we're hungry.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.120
And let's see who's going to win.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.120
Ready, let's go fight.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.910
Let's see who's going to do it because innovation,
speed, innovation, speed, improve, innovation,

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:41.910
innovation, innovation.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.099
How can we improve?

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:47.699
A lot of this comes together and you've got
to be having a level of paranoia on whether

00:06:47.699 --> 00:06:52.220
innovation is still taking place in your company,
or is it business as usual?

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:55.039
#6:Energy, I've talked about this a lot lately.

00:06:55.039 --> 00:06:58.770
You know, studying all of the contentment,
how's your energy, do you still have the same

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:02.050
enthusiasm and excitement, are you getting
too content because you've had way too many

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.050
victories?

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:04.849
It's being paranoid about your own energy.

00:07:04.849 --> 00:07:10.610
Is your energy still at the highest level
of energy where other people are wanting to

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.060
emulate and be around you?

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:22.580
Next, #7, paranoid about the most important
numbers and measures to study.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:27.810
So a lot of times you have, as an entrepreneur,
you can measure hundreds of different numbers.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.169
There are so many different things as an entrepreneur
you can measure.

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:31.169
So many of them.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:32.570
But what's your number one?

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:33.729
What's your number one thing you measure the
most?

00:07:33.729 --> 00:07:36.130
Just think about that?

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.509
Some people say, "The most important thing
to measure is profits?"

00:07:38.509 --> 00:07:39.509
Why?

00:07:39.509 --> 00:07:40.509
Because you're taking profits home?

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:43.090
You know, there's a guy that was working for
an attorney was telling me, "man, you know,

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:46.690
working for this guy who owns this law firm,
every month, profits come in and he takes

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:48.439
all the profits and leaves everything else
behind."

00:07:48.439 --> 00:07:52.500
Well, if your profit is the most important
for you because you're only thinking about

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:53.500
yourself?

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.560
I don't know how many people are excited about
working for that company where the CEOs most

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:57.879
important thing is profits.

00:07:57.879 --> 00:08:01.190
Now this doesn't mean that profits aren't
important because you're going to go out of

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:02.190
business.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.190
Not at all.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:04.289
Profits are important, but it's not number
one.

00:08:04.289 --> 00:08:05.659
Is it top-line revenue?

00:08:05.659 --> 00:08:07.169
Is it gross?

00:08:07.169 --> 00:08:08.419
Is it paying off debt?

00:08:08.419 --> 00:08:09.419
Is it being debt free?

00:08:09.419 --> 00:08:11.419
Is it how much cash you have?

00:08:11.419 --> 00:08:12.419
What is the measure?

00:08:12.419 --> 00:08:13.419
Is it how many employees you have?

00:08:13.419 --> 00:08:14.419
Is it how many vendors you have?

00:08:14.419 --> 00:08:15.419
Is it how many partners you have?

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:16.419
How many products?

00:08:16.419 --> 00:08:17.420
What is it?

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:23.849
To me, the most important measuring stick
is subscribership, to be paranoid about.

00:08:23.849 --> 00:08:30.490
I spoke to a company that came and met with
me, and they said all this stuff that is taking

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:31.490
place with our company.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:34.550
I said, "Tell me how is your subscription
going higher?

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.450
How many subscribers have increased?

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:37.500
How many?

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.399
How many users have increased?"

00:08:39.399 --> 00:08:45.829
So, for instance, let's just say I'm a company
that was accustomed to having 1,000 sales

00:08:45.829 --> 00:08:46.829
per month.

00:08:46.829 --> 00:08:48.810
Hypothetically, I have 1,000 sales per month.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.440
And that 1,000 sales was doing a million dollars
a month for me.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.250
Let's just throw a number out there.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:56.910
Now I'm doing 300 sales per month, but I'm
still doing 1.1 million dollars.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:57.970
I'm very worried.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:00.640
I am very paranoid.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.680
But if I take that 1,000 customers that bought
from me -- they could have bought seven different

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:08.050
products -- but I want to know how many customers
bought from me.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:11.560
1,000 users logged on and bought a product
from me and it's a million dollars?

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:15.480
I don't care about the million dollars, I
want to know how I can get the thousand customers

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.290
buying from me to 2,000 customers buying from
me, even if that million is 1.7 million and

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.000
it's not two million, I'm not worried.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.750
I now have 2,000 customers.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.520
Because I can always improve this.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:27.600
But I can't improve subscribership.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.200
This gives birth to everything else.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:31.910
So I'm very paranoid about subscribership.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.709
I am very paranoid about this area, to measure.

00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:37.780
This gives birth to everything else.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:42.100
What is your number one that you're most paranoid
about, every day that keeps you up at night,

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:43.570
that you wake up with every single morning.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.610
Next one, #8, competition.

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:49.240
Competition really isn't at the top of my
list, but you've got to pay attention to your

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:50.240
competition.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.820
You've got to pay attention to reading the
paper to see these people are coming out with

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.180
this, they're launching this product.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.180
. . you've got to know.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:57.180
Why?

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.589
Is it because everything you do is a reaction
based on what they do?

00:09:58.589 --> 00:10:01.340
No, but you've got to kind of pay attention
to see what they're doing because they're

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:03.930
definitely paying attention to you as well.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:06.420
How high is the level of paranoia on that?

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:11.790
Not that high, because if you're always - Napoleon
always said, they asked him, "which strategies

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.620
do you follow when it comes down to warfare?"

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:14.620
He said, "No one's.

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:15.990
I use my own."

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:17.260
Now that's Napoleon.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:20.279
Everybody followed Napoleon's philosophy and
Alexander's philosophies, right?

00:10:20.279 --> 00:10:26.339
Now secretly, he's probably copying somebody's
but Napoleon was about having certain philosophies

00:10:26.339 --> 00:10:29.950
and action plans in place that maybe nobody
thought about, and everyone wanted to emulate

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:31.220
him, right?

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:35.769
But you've still got to pay attention to the
enemy, they have no 73,000 soldiers.

00:10:35.769 --> 00:10:42.730
This army has 186 tanks, they have 62 jets,
and they have this much nuclear this, you

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:46.320
as a country have to know those things, in
the same way you've got to know your enemies

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.959
have as well at their disposal as much numbers
as you can get.

00:10:48.959 --> 00:10:55.350
#9 - New laws and legislation - every industry,
real estate, insurance, finance, technology,

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:58.260
what are some regulations that are taking
place?

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:02.290
What are some things that maybe a new person
that's going to take an office, how's that

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:04.029
going to effect your business?

00:11:04.029 --> 00:11:08.260
If this party takes over and that party takes
over, if this person gets elected, if that

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:10.600
person gets elected, you kind of need to know
a little bit about that.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:11.600
Yes, you do.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:14.950
You need to be educated about that because
if somebody's a front runner that's going

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:18.740
to be elected and they're telling you, "I'm
going to do this, this, this," and for instance,

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:20.760
imagine if I'm in the health insurance business.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.019
And I know that Obamacare is coming up.

00:11:23.019 --> 00:11:27.220
I spoke to a health-insurance guy yesterday
, 1,000 agents they have, and their business

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:31.740
has gone lower and lower, health insurance
has because it's just a complete different

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:32.740
ballgame.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:36.170
So I'm speaking to health insurance companies,
those who were not prepared for Obamacare,

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:38.100
they took a very big hit.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:44.960
Because Obamacare minimized insurance, commissions,
a lot of different things, and they lost competition,

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:50.480
they raised premiums 39%, I can't afford to
buy this and employees are dropping because

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:53.010
they can't afford so they're going with short-term
medical.

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:57.100
You've got to know that that legislation could
possibly effect your business.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:01.610
If you don't, and you think, "Oh, it's not
going to happen," you've got to assume that

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:02.610
it is going to happen.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:03.910
How are you going to react to it?

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:07.290
Every industry has laws and legislation that
